    "lawCode": "Add §151-j, Ag & Mkts L",
    "lawSection": "Agriculture and Markets Law",
    "memo": " \nBILL NUMBER: S7081\n\nSPONSOR: HINCHEY\n \nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act to amend the agriculture and markets law, in relation to estab-\nlishing the agrivoltaics research program\n\n \nPURPOSE:\n\nThis bill creates the agrivoltaics research program Center for Agrivol-\ntalcs Research, headquartered at New. York State's Land Grant College of\nAgriculture and Life Sciences (Cornell CALS). The purpose of the legis-\nlation is to ensure that In the implementation of the goals of the\nClimate Leadership and Community Protection At for 70% renewable energy\nelectricity generation by 2030, New York State also develops science\nbased strategies to better facilitate the co-location, or dual use, of\nagricultural lands with large and small scale solar arrays. Research at\nCornell University has shown that 40% of New York's existing large scale\nsolar arrays have been constructed on farmland. It can be expected that\nadditional acres of farmland will be sited for solar development over\nthe next several years, and the challenge before the state is to develop\nscience-based recommendations that facilitate the sustainable Intensifi-\ncation of cropping systems for food production, encourage biodiversity,\nand enable the co-location of renewable energy generation.\n\n \nSUMMARY OF SPECIFIC PROVISIONS:\n\nSection 1. Establishes the agrivoltaics research program. This legis-\nlation adds a new paragraph 53 In the general powers and duties of the\nCommissioner, authorizing the establishment of a Center for Agrivoltaics\nResearch and empowering such Center to conduct research on the agricul-\ntural experiment station lands stewarded by Cornell University, as well\nas other locations including private sector sited arrays as deemed\nappropriate for cropping and/or bbdivemity research.\n\nSection 2. This act shall take effect immediately.\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nAgrivoltaics, or the smart usage of land under solar arrays for two\npurposes - growing crops and producing energy- will meet the twin goals\nof bolstering New York's food production In a changing climate while\nreducing tension amongst land use and siting issues in primarily rural\ncommunities during the rapid build out of renewable energy necessary to\nmeet the state's CLCPA goals. Key questions to ask include the types of\nsolar array configurations that will work best for vegetables, small\nfruits, as well as field and perennial forage crops In New York\nsettings. For instance, strip cropping or other to be developed novel\ncropping systems amongst a large-scale solar array may take additional\nland for a large-scale solar installation, but may confer food\nproduction benefits and enable the dual use of the land so that farming\nsystems can continue no matter the soil type. The reality of a large-\nscale solar installation is that both the economic viability of the\ncrop, including labor costs of planting and harvesting, as well as the\nmicroclimate created by the panels needs to be weighed and understood to\nenable efficient agrivoltaics. The Center will focus on the integration\nof digital agriculture (including robotics) to comprehensively look not\nonly at the crops that can grow underneath solar arrays, the configura-\ntions that facilitate agrivoltaics, but also the economics and smaller\nscale machinery/robOtics development that fadlitates actual planting and\nharvesting. Facilitating a consistent investment in R&D funding for a\nfew years to enable the development of holistic agrivoltalcs systems,\nthat work both to improve environmental and economic sustainability,\nwill drastically help facilitate a transition to a renewable energy\neconomy with-profitable farms raising two crops - food and energy.\nAdditionally, this Center will also be tasked with exploring, monitor-\ning, and recommending appropriate siting strategies that encourage\nblodiversity, including grassland bird habitat management s trategies,\nin reflection of the United Nations and the IPCC's renewed focus on the\nconnections between climate change and threats to global biodiversity.\n\n \nLEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\nNew Bill.\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\n\nBudget Request for $1M annually reoccurring investment for 3 years.\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nThis bill shall take effect immediately.\n",
